## qf-psy | Quick-fire: psychology | psych | 0 | - | ps-study
quiz 2 | Pavlov's dogs learned to salivate at…
* A sound associated with food
o The smell of food
o The sight of other dogs
o Darkness
= Classical conditioning. Pavlov used various stimuli including metronomes and buzzers.
> Next

quiz 3 | Confirmation bias is the tendency to…
* Seek and favour information that supports what you already believe
o Remember the first item in a list
o Overrate your own abilities
o Follow the crowd
= It affects scientists too, which is why blinding and pre-registration exist.
> Next

quiz 3 | The "magical number seven, plus or minus two" refers to…
* Short-term memory span
o Hours of sleep needed
o Close friends
o Days to form a habit
= George Miller, 1956. Later research suggests it's closer to about four chunks.
> Next

quiz 3 | The placebo effect is…
* Improvement from believing you're being treated
o A side effect of medicine
o A type of memory error
o A drug interaction
= Placebos can even help when people know they're placebos, in some studies.
> Next

quiz 3 | What is the "halo effect"?
* One good trait making us rate someone's other traits higher
o Feeling better after helping others
o Remembering happy events more
o Overestimating risks
= Attractive people are often rated as more competent.
> Next

quiz 3 | Freud's theory of the mind divided it into…
* Id, ego and superego
o Conscious and subconscious only
o Left and right brain
o Short-term and long-term
= Influential, but largely untestable and not supported in its details.
> Next

quiz 4 | The "Baader–Meinhof phenomenon" is…
* Noticing something everywhere right after learning about it
o Forgetting names instantly
o Believing others share your views
o Hindsight bias
= Also called the frequency illusion.
> Next

quiz 3 | Which part of the brain is most associated with forming new memories?
* Hippocampus
o Cerebellum
o Amygdala
o Medulla
= Patient H.M. lost the ability to form new long-term memories after it was removed in 1953.
> Next

quiz 3 | The "IKEA effect" means…
* We value things more when we've built them ourselves
o We underestimate assembly time
o Cheap furniture lowers happiness
o Choice overload
= Named in a 2011 study involving IKEA boxes and origami.
> Next

quiz 4 | Choice overload: shoppers offered 24 jams vs 6 jams…
* Were less likely to buy when offered 24
o Bought more with 24
o Bought the same
o Only bought the cheapest
= Iyengar and Lepper (2000). Later replications found smaller or mixed effects.
> More quick-fire next

## qf-stat | Quick-fire: statistics | stats | 0 | - | ct-news
quiz 2 | The median of 2, 3, 7, 100 is…
* 5
o 28
o 7
o 3
= Average of the middle two: (3 + 7)/2. The mean is 28: medians resist outliers.
> Next

quiz 3 | A correlation coefficient of −0.9 means…
* A strong negative linear relationship
o No relationship
o A weak positive relationship
o 90% of the variation is unexplained
= r² = 0.81: about 81% of variance explained.
> Next

quiz 3 | Flip a fair coin 5 times. Probability of all heads?
* 1/32
o 1/5
o 1/10
o 1/16
= (1/2)⁵.
> Next

quiz 3 | In a normal distribution, mean, median and mode are…
* All equal
o All different
o Mean > median > mode
o Mode is always zero
= Symmetric distributions line them up.
> Next

quiz 3 | A type I error is…
* A false positive: rejecting a true null hypothesis
o A false negative
o A calculation mistake
o A biased sample
= Type II is a false negative.
> Next

quiz 4 | You roll two dice. Most likely total?
* 7
o 6
o 12
o All equally likely
= Six of the 36 combinations give 7.
> Next

quiz 4 | Regression to the mean predicts that a student who scored exceptionally on one test will probably…
* Score closer to average on the next
o Score even higher
o Score exactly the same
o Score below average
= Part of any extreme score is luck.
> Next

quiz 3 | What's the standard deviation of {5, 5, 5, 5}?
* 0
o 5
o 1
o 20
= No spread at all.
> Next

quiz 4 | 1% of people have a condition. A test catches 90% of cases and has a 9% false positive rate. P(condition | positive)?
* About 9%
o About 90%
o About 50%
o About 1%
= Of 1,000 people: 9 true positives, about 89 false positives. 9/98 ≈ 9%.
> Next

quiz 3 | "Statistically significant at p < 0.05" means the result is…
* Unlikely under the null hypothesis
o Definitely true
o Important
o 95% likely to be true
= It says nothing directly about importance or truth probability.
> More quick-fire next

## qf-cs | Quick-fire: computing | cs | 0 | - | cs-sec
quiz 2 | How many bits in a byte?
* 8
o 4
o 16
o 10
= A byte can represent 256 values.
> Next

quiz 2 | What does CPU stand for?
* Central Processing Unit
o Computer Power Unit
o Central Program Utility
o Core Processing Utility
= It executes instructions.
> Next

quiz 3 | 1111 in binary is what in decimal?
* 15
o 4
o 16
o 1111
= 8 + 4 + 2 + 1.
> Next

quiz 3 | What does HTML stand for?
* HyperText Markup Language
o High Transfer Machine Language
o HyperText Machine Logic
o Home Tool Markup Language
= It structures web pages; CSS styles them.
> Next

quiz 3 | Which data structure works "last in, first out"?
* Stack
o Queue
o Tree
o Hash table
= Like a pile of plates.
> Next

quiz 3 | What is "open source" software?
* Software whose source code is publicly available to use and modify
o Free trial software
o Software without bugs
o Any web app
= Linux, Firefox and Python are open source.
> Next

quiz 4 | Time complexity of looking up a key in a balanced binary search tree?
* O(log n)
o O(1)
o O(n)
o O(n²)
= Each comparison halves the remaining search.
> Next

quiz 3 | What does "RAM" lose when the power goes off?
* Its contents (it's volatile)
o Nothing
o Only half its data
o Its speed
= That's why you save to disk or flash storage.
> Next

quiz 4 | Who is often called the father of theoretical computer science and AI?
* Alan Turing
o Charles Babbage
o John von Neumann
o Steve Jobs
= Turing machines (1936) and the imitation game (1950).
> Next

quiz 3 | Which language did Guido van Rossum create?
* Python
o Java
o C
o Ruby
= First released in 1991, named after Monty Python.
> More quick-fire next

## qf-econ | Quick-fire: economics | econ | 0 | - | ec-hist
quiz 2 | Inflation measures…
* The rise in the general level of prices
o The growth of the stock market
o Unemployment
o Interest rates
= Measured with price indices like CPI.
> Next

quiz 3 | GDP stands for…
* Gross Domestic Product
o General Domestic Price
o Government Debt Percentage
o Gross Debt Product
= Total value of goods and services produced in a country.
> Next

quiz 3 | Who wrote The Wealth of Nations (1776)?
* Adam Smith
o David Ricardo
o John Maynard Keynes
o Karl Marx
= The "invisible hand" appears in it once.
> Next

quiz 3 | Comparative advantage says countries gain from trade even if…
* One country is better at producing everything
o They have the same currency
o They're neighbours
o They have no resources
= Ricardo, 1817: specialise in what you're relatively best at.
> Next

quiz 3 | A monopoly is a market with…
* One seller
o One buyer
o Two sellers
o Many small sellers
= One buyer is a monopsony.
> Next

quiz 4 | If a £10 item rises to £12, by what percentage did the price rise?
* 20%
o 2%
o 12%
o 17%
= 2/10.
> Next

quiz 4 | A "recession" is often defined as…
* Two consecutive quarters of falling GDP
o A stock market drop of 10%
o Inflation above 5%
o Rising unemployment for a month
= A rule of thumb; official bodies use broader measures.
> Next

quiz 3 | What does a central bank do?
* Manages money supply and interest rates
o Collects taxes
o Sets government spending
o Runs shops
= Like the Bank of England or the Federal Reserve.
> Next

quiz 4 | Which economist argued governments should spend more in recessions?
* John Maynard Keynes
o Milton Friedman
o Friedrich Hayek
o Adam Smith
= The General Theory, 1936.
> Next

quiz 4 | A "sunk cost" is…
* A cost already paid that can't be recovered
o A hidden cost
o An investment in shipping
o A future expense
= Rational decisions should ignore it.
> More quick-fire next

## qf-chem | Quick-fire: chemistry | chem | 0 | - | ch-day
quiz 2 | Chemical symbol for gold?
* Au
o Ag
o Gd
o Go
= From the Latin aurum. Ag is silver (argentum).
